## 1. Introduction
As a renewable and environmentally friendly energy resource, hydrogen $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}\right)$ has been one of the promising alternative to fossil fuels.[1–5] Electrochemical water splitting is an effective strategy for the sustainable production of clean $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , which consists of two half reactions, hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER).[6,7] However, the large-scale production of electrochemical $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ system has been impeded due to high overpotential and sluggish reaction rate during the multi-step electron transfer in OER.[8,9] Considering these limitations, it is critical to search the perfect electrode materials with ultra-low cost, high catalytic activity, and long-term durability.[10]
Recently, Fe-based metal organic framework (Fe-MOF) have been attracted extensive attention as an outstanding water splitting electrocatalyst due to its low toxicity, redox activity and the low cost. [11,12] At present, researchers have developed a variety of methods to obtain Fe-MOF, including hydrothermal synthesis, [13,14] microwave radiation,[15,16] stirring method,[17,18] and in-situ growth. The in-situ growth of MOF on conductive substrates is an ideal synthesis strategy with two advantages.[19,20] Firstly, MOFs grown in situ on Fe Foam can be directly used as electrodes, avoiding the formation of dead volumes caused by the introduction of binders. Secondly, the porous characteristics of substrates contribute to the rapid diffusion of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ and $0_{2}$ from the catalyst surface.[21] For instance, Luo et al. fabricated a series of 3D Co/Ni-MOF electrocatalytic materials on Fe Foam by a simple hydrothermal method, and exhibited significantly enhanced OER activity and excellent durability.[22] Li et al. used a novel, economical, and safe preparation method to grow $P{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{3}\mathsf{N@N C}$ NSs/IF in situ on Fe Foam, and it demonstrated excellent water splitting performance and cycle stability.[23]
Meanwhile, depositing metal onto an electrode material surface has also been widely proven to be an effective tactic to further improve the inherent activity of an electrocatalyst.[24] The incorporation of metal with different electronegativity into the lattice could lead to lattice distortion and the electron rearrange, which can accurately change the electronic structure and enhance the intrinsic activity at the atomic level.[25,26] However, most of the reported methods for metal deposition usually involve complicated fabrication steps, which inevitably increase the manufacturing cost and keep it away from practial application.[27] Using electrodeposition method to prepare electrode materials can not only simplify the preparation method but also avoid the activity decline caused by excessive agglomeration of nanoparticles and the ‘‘blocking effect” of polymer binder. [28–31] Therefore, we assume that it is reasonable to combine the above two strtegies to prepare the novel electrocatalysts.
Herein, a series of Fe-MOF/Au composites grown in-suit on Fe Foam were prepared through a hydrothermal and controlled electrodeposition time strategy, in which the Fe Foam acts as both the conductive substrate and a self-sacrificing template.[32–34] The electronic structure of the Fe-MOF/Au/FF composites can be finely adjusted by tailoring the electrodeposition time. The Fe-MOF/Au8/FF nanorods combine the unique advantages of Fe-MOF/FF and Au , which remarkably elevated its efficiency towards OER and HER, and the resulting catalyst delivered $10\ \mathrm{mAcm}^{2}$ current density at the overpotential of $320~\mathrm{mV}$ and $130~\mathrm{mV}$ for OER and HER, respectively. Moreover, when the optimized Fe-MOF/Au-8/
FF composites were employed as dual-function electrocatalysts for overall water splitting, it provided a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ at a battery voltage of only $1.61~\mathrm{V}$ and showed outstanding stability after $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ of uninterrupted continuous operation.
## 2. Experiment
## 2.1. Chemicals
1, 3, 5-Benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\cdotH_{3}B T C}$ , $98\%$ , Macklin), Chloroauric Acid Hydrate $(\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $99.9\%$ , Bide Pharmatech Ltd.), the Fe Foam was purchased from Taobao. Ruthenium (IV) oxide $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , $99\%$ , Bide Pharmatech Ltd.), Platinum Carbon Catalyst (Pt/C, $10\%$ , Bide Pharmatech Ltd.). All chemical reagents were commercially purchased and used without further purification treatment.
2.2. Synthesis of Fe-MOF/FF.
The Fe Foam (FF) was cut into $2\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ squares and washed with dilute hydrochloric acid, ethanol and distilled water. Firstly, 1 mmol of 1, 3, 5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C})$ was dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of distilled water with continuously stirring for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. The solution was transferred to a PTFE lining, and the Fe Foam $(2\times1\times0.1~\mathrm{cm})$ was placed vertically against the inner wall. The lining in was sealed in a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and heated to $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ When lowered to room temperature, the obtained Fe Foam obtained was repeatedly washed with deionized water and ethanol, and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The Fe-MOF gown on Fe Foam was named Fe-MOF/FF.
2.3. Synthesis of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF.
One mmol of tetrachloroalbuminic acid hydrate was weighed and dissolved in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ distilled water. Under the conditions of a voltage window of $_{0-1.3\mathrm{~V~}}$ and a scanning rate of $20\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , the electrodeposition cycles were 1 cycle, 2 cycles, 5 cycles, 8 cycles and 10 cycles. The product was cleaned several times with distilled water and dried at $60\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C$ According to the different number of cycles of electrodeposition, the samples are named as Fe-MOF/ Au-1/FF, Fe-MOF/Au-2/FF, Fe-MOF/Au-5/FF, Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF FeMOF/Au-10/FF (Fe-MOF/Au-X/FF). For comparison, we synthesized a series of $\mathsf{A u/F F}$ materials utilizing the same strategy.
Meanwhile, The mass of the Fe-MOF/Au-1/FF, Fe-MOF/Au-2/FF, Fe-MOF/Au-5/FF, Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF and Fe-MOF/Au-10/FF samples were $3.78~\mathrm{\mg}$ , $3.55~\mathrm{\mg}$ 3.59 mg, $3.62~\mathrm{\mg}$ and $3.89~\mathrm{\mg}$ respectively.
## 3. Results and discussion
The synthetic process and morphological changes of the FeMOF/Au-8/FF nanorods are described in Fig. 1a.[35,36] After the reaction between Fe Foam and three
formic acid to produce Fe-MOF/FF, the Au atoms were electrodeposited on the surface of Fe-MOF/FF. Furthermore, the colour changes of the substrates during Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF synthesis are shown in Fig. 1b. The substrate exhibits significant color at each reaction step and eventually becomes dark brown after electrodeposition of Au. From the molecular structure of Fe-MOFs, it can be seen that Fe-MOF/FF has five coordination centres and a porous structure with $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ as a ligand.
The morphological and structural changes of the materials during the synthesis were observed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and a transmission electron microscope (TEM). Compared with the rough surface of the Fe Foam (FF), the Fe-MOF/FF nanorods grow uniformly on Fe Foam (Fig. S2a, b and Fig. 2a, b). In addition, as shown in Fig. 2f, g, the structure of the nanorods was further proven. Interestingly, after electron deposition, the samples retained the nanorod structure after different electrodeposition cycles, as exhibited in the SEM images (Fig. 2c, d, Fig. S2c-f, and Fig. S3). In addition, we have also observed that the deposition of Au atoms first increased with the increase of electrodeposition cycles, then began to decrease after 8 cycles. This may be due to the decrease of gold atom concentration in the solution. The FeMOF/Au-8/FF nanorods were characterized by TEM and highresolution transmission electron microscopy (HR-TEM). As exhibited in the images Fig. 2h and Fig. 2i, the nanorods exhibit a well-resolved lattice fringe of $0.245\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which corresponds to the orthorhombic Au (200) facet. The SEM image and the Energy dispersive $\mathsf{X}.$ -ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping images of the Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF, exhibited in Fig. 2e, indicate that the C and O atoms of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF are uniformly distributed in the whole nanorod region, and Au atoms are concentrated on the surface of the structures. These phenomena demonstrate that gold atoms were successfully deposited on the Fe-MOF/FF surface. It is worth noting that we have also observed that Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF nanorods are closely connected to each other, resulting in a large amount of void space. Moreover, gold atoms were deposited on the Fe-MOF/FF surface to could further enhance the
conductivity of the material and expose more active specific surface area. This unique open layered porous structure will facilitate electron transport and exposes more active sites, which facilitates the release of hydrogen and oxygen.[37–39]
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns, Fig. 3a, of the prepared samples show several characteristic diffraction peaks. The peaks of the Fe-MOFs are sharp and consistent with those of the Fe-BTC CIF file, indicating that the Fe-BTC coordination was formed successfully. The sharp characteristic peaks indicate that the synthesized sample has excellent crystallinity. In addition, Fouriertransform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy was carried out to ascertain the functional groups of Fe-MOF/FF and Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF (Fig. 3b). The Fe-MOF/FF and Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF have similar spectra. There is a broad and strong absorption peak at $3340\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which is attributed to $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ stretching. The bands arising at 1570 and $1630\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be indexed to the asymmetric stretching vibrations of the carboxylate groups. The peaks arising at 1370 and $1440~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be ascribed to the symmetric stretching vibrations of the carboxylate groups.[40] More importantly, the peak at $546~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to the Fe-O vibration.[41] These results confirm that Fe ions have coordinated with the $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ successfully.
The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) characterization was further used to reveal the composition and surface valence states of Fe Foam, Fe-MOF/FF and Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF, as shown in Fig. 4. The XPS survey spectra of the samples demonstrate that (compared with the Fe-MOF/FF) Fe, O, C and Au elements emerged in Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF, as shown in Fig. S4. It can be further shown that the samples display two deconvoluted C 1 s peaks centered at $284.8\mathrm{eV}$ and $288.3\:\mathrm{eV};$ which are attributed to the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bond of the benzoic ring and the carboxylate group $\scriptstyle(0-C=0)$ ) in $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ , respectively, in $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ , as shown in Fig. 4a.[42] Similarly, the O 1 s spectrum exhibited in Fig. 4c is divided into two peaks centered at $529.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $531.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. The lower binding energy component at $529.9\mathrm{eV}$ is attributed to the $0-C=0$ , while the other peak is assigned to Fe-O.[41] In the high-resolution Fe $\mathsf{2p}$ spectrum. Fig. 4c, two dominating peaks of Fe $2{\tt p}3/2$ and Fe $2{\tt p}1/2$ are observed at $711.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $725.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ , and two shakeup satellites are at 718.3 and $733\ \mathrm{eV}$ , and these indicate that the main existing form of Fe is $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ .[43] Moreover, the XPS peak separation of Au in Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF is observed in Fig. 4d. This may be due the fact that ${\mathsf{A u}}^{3+}$ ions are reduced and firmly adsorbed on Fe-MOF/FF. In addition, compared to the Fe $2{\mathsf{p}}1/2$ peak of Fe Foam at $725.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ , the electron binding energy of this peak in Fe-MOF/FF shifted down to $724.9\mathrm{eV}$ . This is attributed to the formation of Fe-MOF/FF by an Fe Foam coordination reaction with $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ , resulting in redistribution of electron density. The Fe $2{\tt p}1/2$ peak of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF $(724.3\ \mathrm{eV})$ exhibits a negative shift of $0.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ to a lower binding energy compared to that of the Fe-MOF/FF (724.9 eV).[44] This is related to the inter-molecular synergistic Au electrodeposition on Fe-MOF/FF. The results demonstrate that proper deposition of Au can enhance the OER electrocatalytic activity of Fe-MOF/FF derivatives, while too much Au on the Fe-MOF/FF surface will lead to a slight decrease of activity.[37]
The OER electrocatalytic activity of the prepared electrocatalysts was investigated using a three-electrode system. The polarization curves of Fe-MOF/FF and Fe-MOF/Au-X/FF catalysts are shown in Fig. 5a and Fig. S5, and the corresponding overpotentials are $420\mathrm{mV},398\mathrm{mV},357\mathrm{mV},320\mathrm{mV}$ and $340~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , respectively.[45] Moreover, potential plots were drawn according to the $\log{(j)}$ , which can be used to quantify the OER kinetics of the electrocatalyst. As shown in Fig. 5b, the Tafel slope of the Fe-MOF/ Au-8/FF sample is about $51\mathrm{~mV~dec}^{-1}$ , which is much smaller than that of Fe-MOF/FF, Fe-MOF/Au-X/FF, suggesting that appropriate Au deposition could improve the OER electrocatalytic activity of Fe-MOF/FF but excessive Au would lead to a slight decrease in the OER electrocatalytic activity.[46] In addition, chronopotentiometry tests with different current densities from 10 to $100\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ are exhibited in Fig. 5c. The potential of $_{\mathrm{Fe-MOF}/}$ Au-8/FF keeps it steady at each current density even at the high current density of $100\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ . This indicates that Fe-MOF/Au8/FF has excellent electrode stability.[47] Meanwhile, electrocatalytic activity is closely related to resistance, which is a critical indicator of catalyst activity. Fig. 5d shows the Nyquist plots of Fe-MOF/FF and Fe-MOF/Au-X/FF under the overpotential of $320~\mathrm{mV}.$ . Compared with other samples, Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF has the lowest charge transfer resistance, indicating that it has excellent electrochemical performance, which is consistent with the above electrochemical test results.[48] As shown in Fig. 5e, Fe-MOF/Au8/FF exhibits an outstanding long-term electrocatalytic activity within $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ OER testing, and the LSV curves nearly overlap after 3000 cycles (Fig. 5f) reflecting the robust long-term stability and durability of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF in alkaline medium.[49]
The electrochemical activity specific surface area (ECSA) of the prepared electrocatalysts was measured by cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves to investigate the reason why electrodeposition of gold on the Fe-MOF/FF surface can significantly boost the OER activity of Fe-MOF/FF, as shown in Fig. S6. It can be found that the $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}$ and ECSA values of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF are $16.21~\mathrm{\mF~}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $405.25~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which are higher than Fe-MOF/FF $(7.79\ \mathrm{mF}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $194.75~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ , Fe-MOF/Au-1/FF $11.53\mathrm{~mF~cm}^{-2}$ , $288.25~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ), Fe-MOF/Au-2/FF $\cdot13.19\mathrm{\mF\cm}^{-2}$ , $329.75~\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ), Fe-MOF/Au-5/FF $(14.9\ \mathrm{mF}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $372.5~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ), Fe-MOF/Au-10/FF $\cdot13.71\ensuremath{\mathrm{~mF~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-2}$ , $342.5~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ) and $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathsf{F F}$ ( $15.26\mathrm{~mF~cm}^{-2}$ , $381.5~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ). Subsequently, the Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF electrocatalyst still presents the highest activity from the ECSA-normalized LSV curves (Fig. S7). The above results confirmed that the deposition of Au is beneficial for exposing more active sites, thereby further enhancing the electrocatalytic activity of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF.[47]
Furthermore, we evaluated the OER performance of a series $\mathsf{A u}/$ FF materials in alkaline medium, as shown in Fig. S8. The electrocatalyst with the best OER performance is Au-8/FF, which exhibits an overpotential of $330~\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . In the evaluation process of OER when the overpotential reached $320~\mathrm{mV},$ , the fabricated Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF still exhibited the nanorods structure (Fig. S9a, b). It also can be observed that the Fe, C, O and elements are still evenly distributed, and Au is distributed on the surface (Fig. S9c). In addition, the peaks characterized by XRD are roughly the same as the simulated Fe-MOFs peaks (Fig. S12a).
The HER performance of the fabricated samples are also evaluated in Fig. 6. Fig. 6a exhibits the LSV curves of Fe-MOF/FF and FeMOF/Au-X/FF catalysts. After the electrodeposition process, the FeMOF/Au-8/FF shows a high electrocatalytic activity in an alkaline medium (Fig. 6a and Fig. 6b), and it only required a low overpotential $130\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is much higher than that of FeMOF/FF $(305~\mathrm{{mV}})$ ). The results show that the HER catalytic activity of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF is better than other synthesized samples.[50] Meanwhile, the Tafel slope is one of the parameters used to study the reaction pathway and kinetics of the prepared samples (Fig. 6c). The Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF catalyst presents a minimum Tafel slope of $125\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , which is much lower than the values corresponding to Fe-MOF/FF and Fe-MOF/Au-X/FF. These results demonstrate the superior HER kinetics and outstanding electrocatalytic activity of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF for $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution.[51,52] Electrocatalytic stability is a critical standard to be considered for largescale applications in the future. Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF shows excellent rate performance and a fast current response at different current densities, reflecting excellent stability at different current densities for Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF (Fig. 6d). Moreover, the $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ chronopotentiogram and the LSV curve of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF have not changed after the experiment. Fig. 6e, which further proves its extraordinary durability and operational stability.[53] Fig. 6f shows the LSV curves of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF before and after 3000 CV cycles. The change in the electrocatalytic performance of Fe-MOF/Au- $\cdot8/$ FF can be ignored, which further proves the super-stable HER performance.[49]
Subsequently, we estimated the HER of a series Au/FF materials in alkaline solution, as shown in Fig. S10. When the current density is $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , the overpotential of Au-8/FF is $218~\mathrm{mV}$ Compared with Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF electrocatalyst, the performance is slightly inferior. During the evaluation period for HER, the SEM images of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF at the overpotential of $130~\mathrm{mV}$ still presented the nanorod structure (Fig. S11a, b). It reflected that Fe, C, and O elements are still uniformly distributed, and Au is distributed on the surface of the synthesized electrocatalyst, as shown in Fig. S11c. Meanwhile the peaks characterized by XRD were mostly consistent with the simulated Fe-MOFs (Fig. S12b).
In addition, considering the extraordinary OER and HER performance of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF, a two-electrode system was assembled with Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF as both the anode and cathode for overall water splitting, as shown in Fig. 7a.[54] Electrolysis of Fe-MOF/ Au-8/FF only requires small cell voltages of $1.61\mathrm{V}$ to reach the current densities of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for full water electrolysis, as shown in Fig. 7b, reflecting that Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF electrode materials are excellent for overall water splitting performance in alkaline solution.[55,56] Also, the overall water splitting schematic of the FeMOF/Au-8/FF composite electrode material is shown in Fig. S13, shows that water $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}0\right)$ obtains electrons under the polarization of cathode and converts $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ into hydrogen $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}\right)$ . Meanwhile, the generated $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ ions migrate to the anode, where $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ is oxidized in an alkaline medium, thereby converted into oxygen $(0_{2})$ and released.[38] The stability of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF was evaluated by the current
i-t measurement, as shown in Fig. 7c. A stable current of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ was maintained for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , indicating that Fe-MOF/ Au-8/FF has excellent stability in overall water splitting.[57] As a comparison, we investigated the overall water splitting properties of different materials based on Fe (Fig. 7d). The results show that the overall water splitting overpotential of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF is much lower than previously reported Fe-based bifunctional electrodes. The the above results confirm that the Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF electrocatalyst has outstanding overall water splitting properties and durability.[58] Furthermore, we calculated the Faradaic efficiency (FE) to evaluate the conversion efficiency of overall water splitting in energetic the Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF electrocatalyst. The estimated molar mass of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in alkaline solution is about twice that of $0_{2}$ , and the calculated all average FE for overall water splitting after 120 min was approximately $92\%$ as shown Fig. S14. This shows that the Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF electrocatalyst gives high conversion rates and overall has great potential in overall water splitting.[38] The detailed energy storage mechanism and reaction kinetics were further studied on the basis of CV analysis, as shown in Fig. S15. The CV curves of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF at different scanning rates ranged from $30\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ to $70\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . Even at a high scanning rate of $70\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ , all of the curves still kept a similar shape without any obvious change, which is indicates good reversibility and outstanding rate capability (Fig. S15a). The reaction kinetics were investigated by analysing the relationship between the peak current (i) and scan rate $(\nu)$ , with following the formulas:
$$
i=a\nu^{b}
$$
$$
\log i=b\log\upsilon+\log a
$$
In the formulas, $a$ and $b$ are constants with current (i) and scan rate (v), i and $\upsilon$ denote the current density and the scan rate, respectively.[59] The $b$ -value of 0.5 suggested a diffusioncontrolled process, while the $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{b}}$ -value of 1.0 corresponded to a pseudocapacitive controlled process.[60] When the scanning rate is between 30 and $70\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , the b-values of the oxidation peak and reduction peak are 0.7 and 0.75, respectively, indicating that the main contribution is the a pseudo capacitance process (Fig. S15b). The pseudocapacitive contribution was calculated by the equation:
$$
i=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}\nu^{1/2}
$$
Apparently, from the CV (Fig. S15c) curves, it can be found that the that the contributions of diffusion control to capacitance from $30\mathrm{\mV\}s^{-1}$ to $70\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ are $41\%$ , $38\%$ , $35\%$ , $34\%$ and $32\%$ , respectively (Fig. S15d).[61,62] This shows that the contribution of pseudo capacitance gradually increased, while the contribution of the diffusion control process was reduced with an increase in the scanning rate. The results indicated that the pseudo capacitive charge storage mechanism is dominant at a high scanning rate.
The improvement of electrocatalytic activity and kinetics of $\mathsf{A u}/$ Fe-MOF/FF nanorod composites may come from the following three aspects: First, due to the great difference in electronegativity between Fe and Au, there is the strong electron interaction between Fe and Au, which leads to a modification of the electronic structure and makes the adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ reactants and $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ intermediates more active. It will be conducive to reduce the energy barrier in the basic step. Second, the deposition of Au increases the active specific surface area of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF nanorods in contact with the electrolyte, providing more catalytic active sites for water electrolysis. Third, the conductivity of the $_{\mathrm{Fe-MOF}/}$ Au-8/FF composite electrode material can be further enhanced and has smaller charge transfer resistance than the bare Fe-MOF/FF electrocatalyst. This helps ensure electron and mass transfer during water splitting. Therefore, the performance of electrocatalytic water splitting has been greatly improved.[38,44,63]
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we have successfully developed a new strategy for preparation of Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF composites by combining Fe Foam self-sacrificing templates method with electrodeposition. Compared to other synthesis method,[30,31] this work has demonstrated that Fe-MOFs can directly in situ grown on FF surface to avoid the introduction of binder agent, thus reducing the generation of dead volume. Meanwhile, deposition of Au on Fe-MOF/FF surface can adjust the electronic structure and significantly improve the conductivity. After optimization, Fe-MOF/Au-8/FF exhibited efficient overall water splitting performance. This work provides new insights for the design and large-scale practical application of bifunctional electrode materials.
